## Why This Is Asked
Scenario tests Azure-native, region-conscious design with strict data residency, cross-tenant isolation, and cost control.

## Key Concepts
- Region-bound data planes, tenant isolation, data residency, BYOK, DR, cost levers
- Ingest, processing, and serving layers using Event Hubs, Data Lake, Synapse
- Governance via RBAC, Purview, and Key Vault

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo routing function for tenant-scoped storage
function routeEvent(event){
  const t = event.tenantId;
  // route to tenant partition; security checks omitted for brevity
  return storeInTenantPartition(t, event);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement cross-region read-heavy dashboards while preserving residency?
- What failure scenarios would you test and how would you roll back changes without data loss?

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates expertise in designing schema evolution and tenant isolation in Azure data pipelines while balancing flexibility with data integrity.

## Key Concepts

- Schema evolution and backward compatibility
- Per-tenant isolation in Delta Lake on ADLS Gen2
- Lightweight schema registry design
- Data quality gates and validation strategies
- Migration and rollback strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: validate and route incoming record by tenant/version
async function processRecord(record, tenantId) {
  const schema = await getSchema(tenantId);
  const validationResult = validateAgainstSchema(record, schema);
  if (!validationResult.valid) {
    await sendToDeadLetterQueue(record, validationResult.errors);
    return;
  }
  await writeToDeltaLake(record, tenantId, schema.version);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle breaking changes across tenants?
- What monitoring alerts would indicate schema drift or validation failures?
